win32u: Copy presents to offscreen surfaces on a helper thread.

0035 waits for each present to an offscreen surface to complete before
copying it into the window, or the window shows the frame before. The wait
happened on the presenting thread, and a present to a window that is not
mapped completes late: under Xwayland, which signals completion on a timer
for such windows, 26 ms on average, 33 ms at worst, measured on Affinity's
canvas. With FIFO or IMMEDIATE alike, so it is not the vertical blank. Every
canvas frame lost that time; while painting, the canvas updated about 20
times a second and the brush trailed the cursor by about 200 ms.

Hand the wait and the copy to a helper thread. winevulkan starts it once a
device exists and gives it to win32u through a new vulkan_funcs entry,
p_present_worker, which does not return; it is a Wine thread, so it can make
the same copy client_surface_present() made before. vkQueuePresentKHR queues
the copy, replacing a pending one for the same swapchain, and returns.
vkDestroySwapchainKHR drops a pending copy and waits for one in progress.
Until the helper runs, presents wait as before.

The driver interface changes, so WINE_VULKAN_DRIVER_VERSION goes to 49 and
every module that includes vulkan_driver.h must be rebuilt together.

In Affinity under a nested KWin, the brush's lag on screen went from 193-253
ms to 110-122 ms (median over a stroke, three strokes each). The test from
0035 -- a child window presenting a new color once with a vsynced
FLIP_SEQUENTIAL swapchain -- shows the new color on screen with this (3/3),
as with the synchronous wait (2/2), and not without any wait (0/2).
